1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:11,220
Officers sign in with a password and a fresh six-digit code from an
authenticator app on every login. Documents are OCR'd and classified
by a locally fine-tuned AI model, hashed, and anchored on a private
Hyperledger Fabric blockchain -- Verify integrity checks the file
against that anchor.

2
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:22,460
Now an attacker tampers with the stored file directly in the
database, bypassing the application entirely. Verify integrity
immediately catches the mismatch -- and download is refused
outright.

3
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:34,620
Auditors see every action across the system -- including that tamper
alert -- in a tamper-evident, hash-chained audit log, but never
document content. Both the audit trail and the Fabric ledger can be
independently verified end to end, on demand.

4
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:39,180
Rank matters too. A Station Head officer automatically sees every
case at their own station -- not just the ones personally assigned
to them.

5
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:43,820
A Superintendent goes further, overseeing multiple stations at once
-- Central and Northside, both visible here in one list.

6
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:52,100
External reviewers -- forensic labs, judges -- see nothing until a
specific case is explicitly shared with them, and then only read-
only: view, download and verify, never upload or edit.
